  slide "AIPerf: process topology" {
    eyebrow: "AIPerf architecture"
    title: "One coordinator process launches and hosts everything else"
    lede: "The command-line invocation becomes a coordinator process, which starts several internal message-routing proxies inside itself, then forks a handful of always-needed processes as real operating-system processes — and worker processes are spawned dynamically, on command, after the run starts."

    narration: "The coordinator hosts internal proxies and forks the always-needed processes. Workers spawn dynamically, on command, sized by CPU."

    term: {
      word: "coordinator process"
      meaning: "The top-level AIPerf process — what a benchmark invocation actually becomes. It hosts the run's internal message-routing proxies in-process and owns the logic that forks every other service as a real operating-system process, including workers, which it spawns dynamically on command rather than at startup."
    }

    points: [
      "The benchmark invocation becomes the coordinator process; its internal proxies run inside it, not as separate processes.",
      "Always forked as separate processes: dataset loading, timing/dispatch, worker orchestration, results processing.",
      "Worker processes are spawned dynamically on an internal command, not at startup — the coordinator does the actual forking.",
      "A results-processing tier automatically scales its process count alongside the worker count."
    ]

    caption: "One process hosts the proxies and forks the rest — workers spawn on command."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s5-hdr",
        title = "ONE COORDINATOR FORKS THE FLEET",
        caption = "One process hosts the proxies and forks the rest — workers spawn on command.",
